## Independent panel counts and scale

Define `c` as the vertical lattice-line index from the left frame, and `r` as
the horizontal lattice-line index from the top frame. Frame edges are included.
Count each panel separately, not by transferring another panel's dimensions.
Grid-line positions hidden by hatching are not claimed to be individually
visible; count exposed pitches and compare that panel's frame span.

| Panel | Horizontal frame span | Vertical frame span | Axis crossing | Within-panel scale evidence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 26a | 18 cells | 12 cells | `(c9,r6)` | Positive x values 2 and 3 are four and six cells right of the axis; y value 1 is two cells above, and negative y values 1 and 2 are two and four below. |
| 26b | 18 cells | 12 cells | `(c9,r6)` | Nine exposed pitches from left frame to y-axis; axis-to-right-frame has the same nine-pitch span although the far-right lattice is obscured by hatching. The readable x value 1 is two cells right; y values 1 and negative 1 are two cells above/below. |
| 27a | 18 cells | 12 cells | `(c9,r6)` | Exposed grid permits the 18-by-12 count. The x values 1 and 2 are two and four cells right; y value 1 is two cells above. |
| 27b | 18 cells | 12 cells | `(c9,r6)` | Count exposed lattice in the right and lower portions and nine pitches on either side of the y-axis. The x value 1 is two cells right; the upper junction lies two rows above the x-axis beside the upper unit marking. |

Thus one grid cell represents one half-unit in each panel. Eighteen columns
correspond to 19 lattice positions including the frame; twelve rows correspond
to 13 positions. This is not a claim that all 19 positions can be seen through
the dense hatch in 26b. The axis divides each frame into nine horizontal cells
each way and six vertical cells each way.

The coordinate locators used below are grid readings, not printed ordered-pair
labels. The requested points are not printed as named coordinate pairs.

## 26a

The main sloping separator rises from the lower-left to the upper-right. It is
continuous and heavy along its exposed course, including runs several cells
long without a dash cadence. It is a solid source boundary, not a dashed one.
The dense descending-diagonal hatch is on its upper/left side; the lower/right
side retains the thin square grid.

Concrete checks:

- At `(c5,r8)`, four half-unit cells left and two below the origin, the separator
  passes the grid node: supports `(-2,-1)`.
- At `(c9,r6)`, it meets the crossing of the coordinate axes: supports `(0,0)`.
- At `(c13,r4)`, four cells right and two above, it meets the grid node at the end
  of the horizontal unit-height projection: supports `(2,1)`.
- The line cuts the left frame between `r10` and `r11`, and the right frame
  between `r1` and `r2`; it does not end at a frame corner or an integer-y node.
  These edge locations corroborate the three internal readings. Exact
  fractional edge coordinates would be construction, not printed data.

Short horizontal coordinate projections around `r4` and `r8` have separated
dash-like marks distinct from the uninterrupted sloping separator. They must
not be mistaken for a dashed set boundary. The background lattice also has
irregular losses and label interruptions; those do not make it a dashed grid.
The negative x-axis glyph nearest the origin is crowded/merged with strokes.
Its exact typography is not certified or repaired by the node reading.

Disposition: requested three point relationships SUPPORTED; boundary SOLID;
hatch side SUPPORTED; crowded glyph UNREADABLE-NOT-INFERRED.

## 26b

The steep separator rises to the right. Dense descending-diagonal hatch lies
to its right/below side; the left/above side has the exposed thin square grid.
The separator is heavy and continuous between frame contacts, not periodically
broken. It is a solid source boundary.

Concrete checks:

- It crosses the x-axis at `(c10,r6)`, one cell right of the origin, not at the
  two-cell unit mark `(c11,r6)`: supports `(.5,0)`, not `(1,0)`.
- It meets the y-axis at `(c9,r8)`, two rows below the x-axis, adjacent to the
  negative unit-height marking: supports `(0,-1)`.
- At `(c11,r4)`, the boundary reaches the horizontal positive-unit projection:
  two cells right and two above the origin. This corroborates the two intercept
  readings without borrowing an answer or another panel's scale.
- Its visible top-frame contact is at approximately `c13`; its bottom-frame
  contact is at approximately `c7`. These are frame/grid alignment checks,
  not newly printed endpoint coordinates.

The separated horizontal marks at `r4` between the y-axis and boundary are a
coordinate guide, not the separator. There is also a broken short projection
near the negative unit-height marking at `r8`. Do not extend those fragments
into a dashed boundary, or infer a dash style for every obscured grid segment.

Disposition: both requested intercepts SUPPORTED; boundary SOLID; hatch side
SUPPORTED.

## 27a

The circular outline is centered at the axes' crossing. Its four axial extremes
are `(c5,r6)`, `(c13,r6)`, `(c9,r2)`, and `(c9,r10)`, within the thickness of
the printed strokes. Every extreme is four half-unit cells from the origin.
These independent horizontal and vertical checks support radius 2; the nearby
printed positive x value 2 provides an additional check, not the sole evidence.

The two heavy vertical segments occupy `c7` and `c11`: two cells left/right of
the y-axis, hence the cutoffs at x values negative 1 and positive 1. Each is
confined to the disk and terminates at the circular outline, rather than
continuing as a heavy boundary across the whole panel. Their upper contacts
fall between `r2` and `r3`; lower contacts fall between `r9` and `r10`.
No numeric y coordinates are printed at these contacts. Computing exact chord
endpoints would be construction and is not performed here.

Rising-diagonal hatch occupies the left and right circular caps, outward from
the two vertical segments but inside the circle. The middle vertical strip
inside the circle and the area outside the circle show the thin grid without
that cap hatch. Both vertical segments are visibly continuous/solid.

### Local arc style HOLD

Locator: the upper-right quadrant, from just right of the top axial extreme
near `(c9,r2)` toward the upper contact with `c11` and the neighboring arc toward
`(c12,r3)`. The native source has locally thinner, interrupted pieces here.
In contrast, the upper-left, left flank, bottom arc and lower-right arc contain
long, continuous heavy runs. There is no demonstrated circle-wide regular
dash pattern. Nearby thin lattice strokes, especially in the right half, also
show irregular gaps, so reproduction loss is a concrete competing explanation.

Those observations support "predominantly continuous outline with local
interruptions"; they do NOT establish that every local interruption is a
reproduction defect. The single native raster cannot exclude a deliberately
localized dashed arc. It would be plausibility, not pixel proof, to replace
this segment with a certified solid arc, or to certify it as intentional
dashing. The lesson's inclusive convention cannot settle that ambiguity.

HOLD H1: intended solid-versus-dashed status of that upper-right arc segment.
No dependent whole-circle inclusion/style approval is given.

The negative x-axis numeral left of the circle/near the left axial region is
obscured by grid/hatch/stroke interference. Its exact glyph sequence is left
untranscribed. In particular, neither negative cutoff nor radius is justified
by inventing that label; both are independently supported by grid positions.

Disposition: radius 2 and cap cutoffs/cap sides SUPPORTED; vertical boundaries
SOLID; H1 HOLD; obscured negative-axis glyph UNREADABLE-NOT-INFERRED.

## 27b

Two full, heavy straight diagonals cross on the y-axis above the origin. Both
have long continuous runs into the unhatched right half and are solid, not
periodically dashed. Dense steep rising hatch occupies the wedge to the left
of their junction: above the lower/rising separator and below the upper/falling
separator. The other visible sectors retain the thin grid. The lower-left
panel-letter patch interrupts hatch visibility but is not another boundary.

Concrete checks:

- Junction `(c9,r4)` is on the y-axis and two half-unit rows above `(c9,r6)`:
  supports `(0,1)`, not the origin.
- The rising diagonal crosses the x-axis at `(c7,r6)`, two cells left of the
  origin: supports x-intercept negative 1.
- The falling diagonal crosses at `(c11,r6)`, two cells right, aligned with the
  positive unit mark: supports x-intercept positive 1.
- The rising diagonal aligns with top-frame position `c13` and bottom-frame
  position `c1`; the falling one aligns with top-frame `c5` and bottom-frame
  `c17`, within stroke thickness. These exposed continuations agree with the
  junction/intercept grid readings.

The negative intercept is decoded from the lattice, not from an invented
printed negative-unit label. No ordered-pair label is printed at the junction
or at either intercept. No inequality system is constructed in this report.

Disposition: requested junction and both x-intercepts SUPPORTED; both
boundaries SOLID; left-wedge hatch location SUPPORTED.

